kbuild: deb-pkg: build the -dbg package only on request

CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO is on in Ubuntu's configuration, so bindeb-pkg
builds a -dbg package: objcopy --only-keep-debug on every module, a
package of some hundreds of megabytes, which build.sh then throws
away. Make it wait for DEB_DEBUG_PKG in the environment.

scripts/package/builddeb picks what to install from the package name in
debian/control, so dropping the entry is enough for the work not to
happen at all. The kernel image, its modules and the BTF information
inside vmlinux are untouched: this is a packaging switch.

From XanMod's 7.0 series.

--- a/scripts/package/mkdebian
+++ b/scripts/package/mkdebian
@@ -249,7 +249,7 @@
 fi
 fi
 
-if is_enabled CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO; then
+if is_enabled CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO && [ "${DEB_DEBUG_PKG:-}" ]; then
 cat <<EOF >> debian/control
 
 Package: linux-image-${KERNELRELEASE}-dbg
